[direction] d-03 -> closed
**Completed — Assess the routes by which a higgsino becomes visible to direct detection: the mass splittings and the inelastic Z-exchange threshold, LZ inelastic constraints (2609.09830), mixing-induced Higgs couplings in the (mu, M1/M2, tan beta) plane (2606.31013), and the (Omega h^2/0.12) rescaling for sub-thermal higgsinos..** Two of the three success criteria are met; the third is only partly met. (1) The inelastic threshold, worked by hand (f-001): m_Xe = 131 x 0.9315 = 122.0 GeV. The reduced mass mu_A is 108.7 GeV at m = 1.0 TeV and 109.8 GeV at 1.1 TeV. With v_max = 544 + 232 = 776 km/s, (v_max/c)^2 = 6.70e-6, so delta_max = mu_A v_max^2/2 is about 364 keV at 1.0 TeV and 368 keV at 1.1 TeV. The minimum-velocity formula v_min = sqrt(2 delta/mu_A) is Eq. A7 of 2609.09830. (2) Partly met: 2609.09830 does not give an LZ-excluded delta_m0 range (f-002). Its five benchmarks have |mu| = 0.61-1.40 TeV, |M1| and M2 of about 5.8-42 TeV, and one-loop on-shell delta = 8.9-36.4 keV. They predict at most about 1.03 combined NLO events in LZ, need 9.4-65.5 t-y of exposure to be probed (Table VII, Fig. 6), and are all consistent with LZ. NLO vertex corrections can exceed 100 percent, and in BP2 the inelastic cross section is about twice the LO elastic one. Splittings of O(10) keV with gaugino masses of O(10) TeV arise only from partial cancellation between the M_Z^2/M1 and M_Z^2/M2 terms when M1 and M2 have opposite signs; generically the paper quotes O(10-100) MeV. My own estimate, using the formula that is not yet sourced (q-05): same-sign M2 = 2 M1 would need M1 of about 1.4e7 GeV to bring delta below 364 keV. Inelastic Z-exchange therefore matters only in tuned corners. (3) Mixing region where elastic SI becomes visible (f-003, 2606.31013): Higgs exchange gives f_q ~ -(g^2/8M_h^2)[1/(M2-|mu|) + tan^2 theta_W/(M1-|mu|)](1 + sgn(mu) sin 2beta). This is suppressed at large gaugino mass and at small tan beta with mu<0. The scan covers 150<|mu|<1000 GeV, 1.6<tan beta<50 and 0.5<M1<10 TeV with M2 = 2 M1. Direct detection is sensitive where mixing produces larger charged-neutral splittings and reaches the thermal mass of about 1 TeV (Figs. 4 and 5 of that paper; the figures were not extracted). The pure-limit radiative charged-neutral splitting is about 356 MeV (Eq. 13 of 2606.31013), rather than the 340 MeV in the brief. (4) Rescaling: 2606.31013 Eq. 38 uses sigma_eff = (Omega_H h^2/0.12) sigma, computed with micrOMEGAs 6.3.0, and 2609.09830 states that limits weaken proportionally for sub-thermal fractions. Quotes from both papers are cited in /Users/nramachandra/hep-genesis-testing/projects/ramp-research-higgsino-dd/runs/261016_110437/references.bib. Full texts are at the same run directory as 2609.09830.txt and 2606.31013.txt.
