**Finding:** Correction to f-008: an upper bound below 1e-48 cm^2 compared with the LZ minimum of 2.2e-48 cm^2 (at 40 GeV) is a factor of more than 2.2 (more than 0.34 decades), not 2 orders of magnitude. The comparison that actually matters is at the same mass. At 1.1 TeV the linearly rescaled LZ limit of about 6e-47 cm^2, divided by the bound of 1e-48 cm^2, gives a ratio of at least about 60 (at least about 1.8 decades). That ratio is still an estimate: the LZ and XENONnT curves at 1 TeV were not read.
